#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499583} :activity/id #uuid "f2bc7a10-1a64-5702-870d-eb64ad2d650c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050705 :topic/id #uuid "6047b0c9-9ae0-5fc8-9179-8ce5172d5c9d" :topic/math-academy-id 1998} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499568 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d59625-eac9-589e-ab14-341551eea08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371915} {:answer/id #uuid "7b889d37-5d45-5818-9d27-40379a4533ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371916} {:answer/id #uuid "b06248ff-2151-5554-b5af-ab842ba5705a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371917} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e1a6f5-fc15-5138-88ce-fdee68bb88d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371918} {:answer/id #uuid "5b52d6ac-ffd9-5f34-ba9e-30bc4a86d030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371916} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ac9b17-c604-511f-9b48-3b87989affec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75940fb8-f294-5aab-8f7b-cfe4e75211a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160891" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{3x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{xy}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{2x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/05/05d2ca47db245b147cb52f95a474a60ece144931c8b6e1e40c6628a99b53d690.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y} & -\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy & \\text{ }\\text{ }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=y=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}=\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "01bfa9b8-ea54-5e15-adea-d773f4a9fc72"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499571 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76d7d05-c899-566f-a267-b260f4f22494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373341} {:answer/id #uuid "e610ef2b-0037-56d0-bead-5ff13607271a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373342} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed3876d-8c80-5bc3-9185-9669f13f9f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{32}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373343} {:answer/id #uuid "639ae9f3-3123-5bb0-aa7c-f883873eebe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373344} {:answer/id #uuid "8823d84a-4e7a-5110-b70c-63b3e1900363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{47}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373343} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3452f16-065c-5d3f-b342-bf97b568f3a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "672b45e6-0aa1-59c9-9e64-dd97d59af187" :question/math-academy-id "q-83947" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curve $(y+3x)^{2}-x=3$ and the line $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+3x,\\,v=x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+3x=u$ and $x=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3x)^{2}-x=3\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=3\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{2}-3 \\\\ v=1\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}u=\\pm 2 \\\\ v=1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(\\pm 2,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,{u}^{2}-3\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/26/2687f0cc14eb0b476ea5f4ba7272e7f248bb7dcb276a0e2b0c39e551501b011d.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(-1)^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}[v]_{{u}^{2}-3}^{1}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(1-({u}^{2}-3))\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}(4-{u}^{2})\\,du \\\\  & ={[4u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{16}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0aa5fab4-668b-577d-91a3-ac72b6d14200" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b9b4ef9-8eb6-5c5c-b433-5ffc4c7236ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498824} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8c6c00-5b20-53bc-acb7-b0b31ab2298d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5}{12})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498947} {:answer/id #uuid "88676aa6-a08b-583a-a6f9-2618bd726361" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{12}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499034} {:answer/id #uuid "e1e226dc-7e0d-534a-9b5e-f226b2636d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{12}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499239} {:answer/id #uuid "f378af41-a686-5292-9362-123eea7e70ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(60)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499239} :answer-field/id #uuid "fecc3609-91c7-5f66-a205-ec1648ad9bd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "77ed6de7-60cd-57e5-95ef-8b1b61a0f9cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-160638" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{{e}^{x}},\\,y=\\frac{12}{{e}^{x}},\\,2y-1=0,\\,2y-5=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}y=5,\\,{e}^{x}y=12,\\,2y=1,\\,2y=5\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={e}^{x}y,\\,v=2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={e}^{x}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {e}^{x}y=5\\,\\text{ }\\,⟹\\,u=5 \\\\  & {e}^{x}y=12\\,⟹\\,u=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 2y=5\\,⟹\\,v=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,5\\le u\\le 12,\\,1\\le v\\le 5\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/61/615d6822e60e9b5ff646497e14df1468e1fd09ee67fab5c334b635d130f16f14.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}y & {e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}y \\\\  & =2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{2u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{12}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5))\\cdot (5-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{12}{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2fc3ef89-3fe1-5a0b-b8af-8112d233e0f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499583 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499479 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17418eda-e303-583e-abb8-8163f34cf65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498779} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1f2bf6-1e84-5da8-8223-f7a8617abe63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa17f5a-3932-5a85-be4a-9e7f8aca50c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499103} {:answer/id #uuid "af9d5967-0e62-5e9c-80be-bb9563606efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499116} {:answer/id #uuid "e507ffc4-50dc-56ba-8db0-2ce573a2305a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499248}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186498865} :answer-field/id #uuid "06575685-8b6e-5313-b708-d919a2eb705b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "5e32ec93-9a95-5b63-b1e8-8733a4d5c7af" :question/math-academy-id "q-82910" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the curves $(x+y)^{3}-y=0$ and $(x+y)^{2}-y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{3}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{3}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $x+y=u$ and $y=v$ into the equation of the second curve, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,{u}^{2}-v=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}v={u}^{3} \\\\ v={u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}(u-1)=0 \\\\ v=u.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{3}\\le v\\le {u}^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a8d2549c665a918cc8d69c69136fcb49e654ebb9aa5e53611155798e791b36a9.png)\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=(1)^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,dv]du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}v{|}_{{u}^{3}}^{{u}^{2}}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({u}^{2}-{u}^{3})du \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{u}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "486db070-d151-53d7-9850-f2f093b172d4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499571}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccecda64-e614-576a-95ef-e05124675fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371888} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dbc0ee-bd56-5d09-82ae-0a50d663a617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371889} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1c272c-a26e-56c4-b420-6c0e9237be97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371890} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9488a7-26bc-5807-abd0-f5550f336827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371891} {:answer/id #uuid "49a9bf82-6b9b-5af5-bd83-8f2fc3e179b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371892}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371890} :answer-field/id #uuid "f732fda0-f223-5e8e-9cd7-3bad366f7805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1533ae23-2a8c-5746-8cc6-278953144598" :question/math-academy-id "q-160857" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n3y=4x+1,\\,3y=4x-2,\\,3x=2y+1,\\,3x=2y+3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n4x-3y=-1,\\,4x-3y=2,\\,3x-2y=1,\\,3x-2y=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=4x-3y,\\,v=3x-2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=4x-3y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4x-3y=-1\\,⟹\\,u=-1 \\\\  & 4x-3y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3x-2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 3x-2y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/5d/5dc4be989300dca3e52b7ff1dfb6a21c60409976fe55d42f5ae530f033725a64.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-8+9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a2e7eed7-b052-54ef-bf9d-8a28ecab7ca6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499568}}] :activity/title "Computing Areas Using a Change of Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498730}]}
